# Cursor Rules for Go Articles Project

## Project Context
This is a Go articles repository containing educational content about Go concurrency patterns, synchronization techniques, and best practices.

## Code Style & Standards
- Use Go 1.22+ standard library features
- Follow idiomatic Go patterns and best practices
- Prefer composition over inheritance
- Use interfaces for abstraction
- Always handle errors explicitly
- Use context for cancellation and timeouts
- Prefer `sync` package primitives for specific synchronization needs
- Use channels for communication between goroutines

## Architecture Guidelines
- Keep code simple and readable
- Write short, focused functions with single responsibility
- Avoid global state; use dependency injection
- Use defer for cleanup operations
- Implement proper error wrapping with context

## Testing
- Write unit tests using table-driven patterns
- Use `go test -v` for verbose test output
- Aim for high test coverage

## Documentation
- Write clear comments for exported functions
- Use GoDoc-style documentation
- Include examples in code when helpful

## File Structure
- Keep examples in separate `.go` files when needed
- Markdown files contain educational content with code examples
- Use `go.mod` for dependency management

## Concurrency Patterns
- Prefer channels for communication between goroutines
- Use `sync.WaitGroup` for waiting on multiple goroutines
- Use `sync.Mutex` or `sync.RWMutex` for protecting shared state
- Use `sync.Once` for one-time initialization
- Use `sync.Cond` for condition-based synchronization

## Agent Preferences
- When showing code examples, extract them to runnable `.go` files
- Explain Go concurrency concepts clearly
- Provide working, complete examples
- Test all code examples before including them



